   "source": [
    "# 09 — Target Variable Analysis: Liquidation as a Default Proxy\n",
    "\n",
    "**Motivation (professor feedback):**  \n",
    "> *"The model predicts Aave liquidation risk, which is not exactly the same as borrower default or general creditworthiness."*\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook makes that distinction explicit and defends why liquidation is nevertheless a **valid and informative proxy** for DeFi credit risk — while documenting its limitations clearly.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What this notebook covers\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Conceptual mapping**: Aave V2 liquidation vs traditional credit default\n",
    "2. **Empirical defense**: do liquidated wallets show systematically different on-chain behavior?\n",
    "3. **Proxy quality analysis**: feature separability between default (1) and non-default (0)\n",
    "4. **Abandoned position detection**: a complementary default signal not captured by liquidation alone\n",
    "5. **Limitations and scope** for honest reporting\n",
    "\n",
    "### Literature context\n",
    "\n",
    "Using protocol-level liquidation events as a default proxy is standard in DeFi credit research:\n",
    "- Gudgeon et al. (2020) *\"DeFi Protocols for Loanable Funds\"* — uses collateral seizure as default event\n",
    "- Perez et al. (2021) *\"Liquidations: DeFi on a Knife-Edge\"* — analyzes Aave/Compound liquidation structure\n",
    "- Xu & Vadgama (2022) *\"From Banks to DeFi\"* — maps liquidation to Basel III credit event definitions\n",
    "\n",
    "The key insight: in DeFi, **liquidation is the closest observable analog to default** because there is no explicit repayment schedule — the protocol enforces solvency mechanically rather than contractually."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import sys\n",
    "sys.path.insert(0, '..')\n",
    "\n",
    "import warnings\n",
    "warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n",
    "\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import matplotlib.patches as mpatches\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "from scipy import stats\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.set_theme(style='whitegrid', context='notebook')\n",
    "BLUE  = '#185FA5'\n",
    "RED   = '#C0392B'\n",
    "GREEN = '#2E9E6B'\n",
    "GREY  = '#8A9BB4'\n",
    "\n",
    "PALETTE = {0: GREEN, 1: RED}  # 0=non-default (green), 1=default (red)\n",
    "\n",
    "print('Libraries loaded.')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Conceptual mapping: Aave liquidation vs traditional default\n",
    "\n",
    "| Dimension | Traditional credit default | Aave V2 liquidation |\n",
    "|-----------|---------------------------|---------------------|\n",
    "| **Trigger** | Missed scheduled payment | Health factor < 1.0 (collateral ratio too low) |\n",
    "| **Mechanism** | Legal contract breach | Algorithmic enforcement (smart contract) |\n",
    "| **Agent** | Borrower stops paying | Market price moves (or borrower adds no collateral) |\n",
    "| **Involuntary?** | Always involuntary | Involuntary — borrower loses collateral to liquidator |\n",
    "| **Financial loss?** | Lender loses principal | Borrower loses collateral at a discount (5–15% penalty) |\n",
    "| **Predictability** | Behavioral + macroeconomic signals | On-chain behavioral signals + collateral price |\n",
    "| **Verifiability** | Credit bureau reports | Publicly auditable on-chain events |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why liquidation IS a valid default proxy\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Involuntary loss of collateral** = financial distress, regardless of mechanism\n",
    "2. **Behavioral precursors are detectable**: over-leveraging, low repay-to-borrow ratios, burst activity before event\n",
    "3. **The model learns behavior, not price**: features are wallet behavior metrics, not asset prices — so the model captures *who* takes on excess risk, not *when* markets crash\n",
    "4. **Consistent with Basel III spirit**: Basel defines default as \"obligor unlikely to pay\" — a liquidated DeFi borrower who lost collateral fits this definition\n",
    "\n",
    "### What it does NOT capture\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Market-crash liquidations**: a wallet with 150% collateral ratio during ETH -40% crash may be liquidated despite responsible borrowing behavior. Our behavioral features help separate these.\n",
    "2. **Abandoned positions**: wallets that borrowed and stopped interacting (no repayment, position eventually expires or becomes insolvent) — not always captured by Aave V2 liquidation events\n",
    "3. **Cross-protocol defaults**: a wallet may default on Compound or MakerDAO but not Aave"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Load data\n",
    "df = pd.read_parquet('../data/processed/feature_matrix.parquet')\n",
    "liq = pd.read_parquet('../data/raw/aave_v2_liquidations.parquet')\n",
    "\n",
    "liq['dt'] = pd.to_datetime(liq['timestamp'], unit='s')\n",
    "liq['year_month'] = liq['dt'].dt.to_period('M')\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f'Feature matrix: {df.shape[0]:,} wallets')\n",
    "print(f'  Default (liquidated): {df[\"label\"].sum():,} ({df[\"label\"].mean():.1%})')\n",
    "print(f'  Non-default:          {(df[\"label\"]==0).sum():,} ({(df[\"label\"]==0).mean():.1%})')\n",
    "print(f'\\nLiquidation events: {len(liq):,}')\n",
    "print(f'  Date range: {liq[\"dt\"].min().date()} → {liq[\"dt\"].max().date()}')\n",
    "print(f'  Unique borrowers: {liq[\"borrower\"].nunique():,}')\n",
    "\n",
    "events_per_wallet = liq.groupby('borrower').size()\n",
    "print(f'\\nLiquidation events per wallet:')\n",
    "print(f'  Mean:   {events_per_wallet.mean():.1f}')\n",
    "print(f'  Median: {events_per_wallet.median():.0f}')\n",
    "print(f'  Max:    {events_per_wallet.max()}')\n",
    "print(f'  >1 liquidation: {(events_per_wallet > 1).sum()} wallets ({(events_per_wallet > 1).mean():.1%})')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Liquidation event timeline\n",
    "\n",
    "Visualizing when liquidations occurred helps identify if events cluster around market crashes\n",
    "(which would indicate market-driven rather than behavior-driven defaults)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "monthly_liq = liq.groupby('year_month').size().reset_index(name='events')\n",
    "monthly_liq['year_month_dt'] = monthly_liq['year_month'].dt.to_timestamp()\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(13, 4))\n",
    "ax.bar(monthly_liq['year_month_dt'], monthly_liq['events'], width=25,\n",
    "       color=RED, alpha=0.7, label='Liquidation events')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Mark known market crash events\n",
    "crashes = [\n",
    "    ('2021-05-19', 'Crypto\\ncrash May\\'21'),\n",
    "    ('2022-05-12', 'LUNA\\ncollapse'),\n",
    "    ('2022-11-08', 'FTX\\ncollapse'),\n",
    "    ('2023-03-11', 'USDC\\ndepeg'),\n",
    "]\n",
    "for date_str, label in crashes:\n",
    "    dt = pd.Timestamp(date_str)\n",
    "    ax.axvline(dt, color='orange', ls='--', alpha=0.8, lw=1.5)\n",
